/***********************************************************************************************************************
da4_gethandlersourcedetail.probesql -- dbo.uspGetHandlerSourceDetail (script 505).

Run: sqlcmd -S localhost -E -C -I -d RCRAInfo -y 0 -i "C:\~MDE-55TT2J4\RCRAInfo\build\tmp\da4_gethandlersourcedetail.probesql"
-I is REQUIRED. dbo.HandlerSource carries a filtered unique index (UX_dbo_HandlerSource_Natural), and any write to a
table with one needs SET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER ON or the insert fails with error 1934 naming indexed views.

WHAT THIS PROBE IS FOR. 505 is the one read that RETURNS the contact name, phone number, email address and mailing
address that 503 and 504 withhold. Its header makes four claims about why that is acceptable and one claim about why it
reads the history view rather than dbo.vwHandlerSource. This file's job is to turn as many of those sentences as
possible into something a run can fail on -- in particular section L5, which asserts that the PII in the RESULT SET
never reaches logs.ExecutionLog, the table the monitoring web app can read.

CONVENTIONS THIS FILE FOLLOWS, ALL OF THEM LOAD-BEARING:

  * Every fixture row uses an MDPROBE0505% HandlerId, so the reset and the cleanup can find them without touching a
    real handler.
  * SOFT DELETE ONLY. There is no hard delete anywhere in this database, so cleanup sets IsDeleted = 1 and the fixture
    rows stay. The reset at the top retires anything a previous run left visible, which is what makes this file
    re-runnable.
  * REFUSALS use plain EXEC and successes use INSERT ... EXEC. That is not stylistic: INSERT ... EXEC opens a
    transaction, and a procedure that throws inside one leaves the transaction doomed (error 3930), so the CATCH cannot
    write its log row and the section L assertions would measure nothing.
  * INSERT ... EXEC IS ITSELF AN ASSERTION. #Detail carries all 215 columns in the procedure's order, so a change to
    the projection's list, order or types fails the insert rather than passing silently -- and because a SECOND result
    set of a different shape would also fail it, every success below is evidence that 505 returns exactly one.
    INSERT ... EXEC also succeeds on an EMPTY result set and fails on a MISSING one, which a row count cannot tell
    apart, and that distinction is the whole point of A4 and A5.
  * #Detail IS BUILT WITH SELECT TOP (0) ... INTO RATHER THAN A HAND-WRITTEN CREATE TABLE. 215 type declarations typed
    by hand is a transcription error waiting to happen, and the types have to be exact or the insert reports a shape
    change that never occurred. ISNULL on the first column is not decoration: SELECT ... INTO propagates the IDENTITY
    property through the view -- measured, tempdb.sys.columns reports is_identity = 1 on the copy -- and INSERT ... EXEC
    supplies no column list, so the insert would fail with error 8101 instead of asserting anything. Wrapping the column
    in an expression breaks the propagation and keeps the type INT.
  * HandlerSourceId IS AN IDENTITY, SO THE IDS ARE CAPTURED, NOT ASSUMED OR LOOKED UP. #Ids holds one row per fixture
    version, each taken from SCOPE_IDENTITY () immediately after its INSERT, and every assertion below takes its argument
    from there. Looking them up afterwards by HandlerId and Sequence is the obvious alternative and it is wrong -- see
    the comment on the fixture. Temp tables survive across GO on one connection; local variables do not, which is why
    #Ids exists at all rather than four variables.
  * ONE WARNING LINE IS EXPECTED, and only one. Section X1 deliberately calls the procedure inside a doomed transaction,
    and logs.uspRecordExecutionError's severity-10 warning about error 3930 is the visible form of the G37 boundary X1
    exists to measure. A run that prints that warning anywhere ELSE means an assertion wrapped a THROW in a transaction
    it did not mean to open.
  * THE LOG ASSERTIONS ARE RELATIVE TO A BASELINE. logs.ExecutionLog is append-only and cleanup does not touch it, so a
    second run of this file would double any absolute count. #Base holds the highest ExecutionLogId that existed before
    section A, and every count in section L is taken above it.

WHAT THE FIXTURE IS. Three handlers, each one existing to make a different claim falsifiable:

  HANDLER A -- MDPROBE05051, THE SUBJECT, and the only one carrying contact details. Two visible versions:

  Label  Seq  CurrentRecord  HandlerName       Contact                             Mailing
  A1     1    0              OLD DETAIL CO     ZZPROBEFIRST ZZPROBELAST            99 ZZPROBE MAILING WAY, PA
  A2     2    1              NEW DETAIL LLC    ZZPROBEFIRSTB ZZPROBELASTB          98 ZZPROBE OTHER WAY, MD

  A1 IS SUPERSEDED (CurrentRecord = 0) AND IT IS THE MORE IMPORTANT OF THE TWO. It is the row 504's version list shows
  and 503's grid cannot: section B2 asserts 505 returns it, and section B3 asserts dbo.vwHandlerSource returns nothing
  for the same id. Together those two are the measured form of 505's claim that reading the history view is what makes
  504's list clickable -- had it read dbo.vwHandlerSource, B2 would return an empty set and the two procedures would
  each be correct alone and useless together.
  A1's contact values are SENTINELS -- distinctive strings that appear nowhere else in this database -- so that section
  B5 can prove the columns really are returned and section L5 can prove the same strings never reach the log.
  A1's mailing state is 'PA'. Out-of-state MAILING addresses are expected by MDE's data-scope decision, and the
  assertion in B6 is that the value survives the projection unmolested. Note what it is NOT: dbo.HandlerSource carries
  ZERO check constraints, measured, so nothing in the schema was ever going to reject 'PA'. The MD-only rule applies to
  activityLocation and is enforced in the loader; this assertion pins the read, not the rule.

  HANDLER B -- MDPROBE05052. ONE version, SOFT-DELETED. Its HandlerSourceId is real and known, and 505 must return an
  EMPTY RESULT SET for it (A5). That is AR7 arriving through the view, with the procedure never naming IsDeleted.

  HANDLER C -- MDPROBE05053. ONE version with CurrentRecord NULL -- never stamped by dbo.uspReconcileCurrentRecord.
  Invisible to 503's grid, reachable here, and B4 asserts the NULL is returned rather than coerced.

WHAT THIS PROBE CANNOT ASSERT, STATED HERE BECAUSE IT MATTERS TO MDE's REQUIREMENT. Section A0 calls the procedure with
NO arguments. That fails with error 201 before the procedure body executes, so the TRY/CATCH inside the procedure never
runs and NOTHING IS LOGGED. It is the one refusal this procedure structurally cannot record, and A0b asserts exactly
that -- the log is unchanged -- rather than pretending otherwise.

WHAT THIS PROBE DELIBERATELY LEAVES TO A GUARDRAIL. Section C2 compares the projected names against sys.columns, which
is the same comparison build/check_detail_projection.py makes. The duplication is intentional and the two are not
redundant: this file asserts it of the DEPLOYED procedure on a developer workstation, and the guardrail asserts it of
the FILE on every run including CI, where this probe does not run.

A CLEAN RUN IS: 25 PASS, no FAIL, no CHANGED, no Msg line, and exactly one warning -- X1's. Verified twice in a row,
with identical output, on 2026-09-05.
***********************************************************************************************************************/

SET NOCOUNT ON;
SET XACT_ABORT OFF;   -- the refusal sections need the batch to continue after a caught error
GO

PRINT N'=== da4_gethandlersourcedetail: dbo.uspGetHandlerSourceDetail ===';
GO

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Reset. Retire anything a previous run left visible. Soft delete only.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE dbo.HandlerSource
   SET IsDeleted             = 1
     , auditDeletedBy        = ORIGINAL_LOGIN ()
     , auditDeletedDateUtc   = SYSUTCDATETIME ()
     , auditModifiedBy       = ORIGINAL_LOGIN ()
     , auditModifiedDateUtc  = SYSUTCDATETIME ()
 WHERE HandlerId LIKE N'MDPROBE0505%'
   AND IsDeleted = 0;
PRINT CONCAT (N'reset: retired ', @@ROWCOUNT, N' leftover fixture row(s).');
GO

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Fixture. One statement per row, and each id captured with SCOPE_IDENTITY () on the line after its INSERT.
--
-- THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE IS WRONG, AND IT FAILED HERE BEFORE THIS COMMENT EXISTED. Looking the ids back up afterwards
-- by HandlerId and Sequence -- even narrowed to rows created in the last few minutes -- matches the rows PREVIOUS RUNS
-- left behind, because cleanup is a soft delete and those rows are still in the table. A second run within the window
-- collides on #Ids's primary key, #Ids ends up empty, and every assertion below then calls the procedure with NULL and
-- reports the refusal message as its own failure. SCOPE_IDENTITY () names the row this statement just inserted and
-- nothing else, so it is immune to every leftover.
--
-- SCOPE_IDENTITY () only holds inside the batch that did the insert, so the whole fixture is ONE batch: #Ids is created
-- first, the four inserts follow, and there is no GO until all of them are captured.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE #Ids (Label NVARCHAR (10) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, HandlerSourceId INT NOT NULL);

-- A1. SUPERSEDED, and the row carrying the sentinel contact details. The mailing state is out-of-state on purpose.
INSERT INTO dbo.HandlerSource
    (HandlerId, ActivityLocation, SourceType, Sequence, CurrentRecord, IsDeleted, SourceTypeDescription, HandlerName
   , ReceivedDate, SrcUpdatedDate, SrcUpdatedBy, SiteLocationAddress1, SiteLocationCity, SiteLocationStateCode
   , SiteLocationZip, ContactFirstName, ContactLastName, ContactPhone, ContactEmail
   , SiteMailingAddressAddress1, SiteMailingAddressCity, SiteMailingAddressStateCode, SiteMailingAddressZip)
VALUES (N'MDPROBE05051', N'MD', N'A', 1, 0, 0, N'Notification', N'OLD DETAIL CO'
      , '2023-03-01', '2026-03-01', N'ZZPROBE.EPAUSER', N'1 First St', N'Baltimore', N'MD'
      , N'21201', N'ZZPROBEFIRST', N'ZZPROBELAST', N'5555550199', N'zzprobe.contact@example.invalid'
      , N'99 ZZPROBE MAILING WAY', N'Philadelphia', N'PA', N'19104');
INSERT INTO #Ids (Label, HandlerSourceId) VALUES (N'A1', CAST (SCOPE_IDENTITY () AS INT));
-- A2. The CURRENT version. Different contact values, so B5 cannot pass by reading the wrong row.
INSERT INTO dbo.HandlerSource
    (HandlerId, ActivityLocation, SourceType, Sequence, CurrentRecord, IsDeleted, SourceTypeDescription, HandlerName
   , ReceivedDate, SrcUpdatedDate, SrcUpdatedBy, SiteLocationAddress1, SiteLocationCity, SiteLocationStateCode
   , SiteLocationZip, ContactFirstName, ContactLastName, ContactPhone, ContactEmail
   , SiteMailingAddressAddress1, SiteMailingAddressCity, SiteMailingAddressStateCode, SiteMailingAddressZip)
VALUES (N'MDPROBE05051', N'MD', N'A', 2, 1, 0, N'Notification', N'NEW DETAIL LLC'
      , '2025-03-01', '2026-04-01', N'ZZPROBE.EPAUSERB', N'2 Second St', N'Baltimore', N'MD'
      , N'21202', N'ZZPROBEFIRSTB', N'ZZPROBELASTB', N'5555550200', N'zzprobe.contactb@example.invalid'
      , N'98 ZZPROBE OTHER WAY', N'Baltimore', N'MD', N'21202');
INSERT INTO #Ids (Label, HandlerSourceId) VALUES (N'A2', CAST (SCOPE_IDENTITY () AS INT));
-- B1. SOFT-DELETED on insert. A real HandlerSourceId that must come back as an empty result set.
INSERT INTO dbo.HandlerSource
    (HandlerId, ActivityLocation, SourceType, Sequence, CurrentRecord, IsDeleted, SourceTypeDescription, HandlerName
   , ReceivedDate, SrcUpdatedDate, SiteLocationAddress1, SiteLocationCity, SiteLocationStateCode, SiteLocationZip)
VALUES (N'MDPROBE05052', N'MD', N'A', 1, 0, 1, N'Notification', N'GONE DETAIL CO'
      , '2024-03-01', '2026-05-01', N'3 Third St', N'Rockville', N'MD', N'20850');
INSERT INTO #Ids (Label, HandlerSourceId) VALUES (N'B1', CAST (SCOPE_IDENTITY () AS INT));
-- C1. NEVER STAMPED. CurrentRecord NULL, so 503's grid cannot see it and only 504 and 505 can.
INSERT INTO dbo.HandlerSource
    (HandlerId, ActivityLocation, SourceType, Sequence, CurrentRecord, IsDeleted, SourceTypeDescription, HandlerName
   , ReceivedDate, SrcUpdatedDate, SiteLocationAddress1, SiteLocationCity, SiteLocationStateCode, SiteLocationZip)
VALUES (N'MDPROBE05053', N'MD', N'A', 1, NULL, 0, N'Notification', N'UNSTAMPED DETAIL CO'
      , '2024-09-01', '2026-06-01', N'4 Fourth St', N'Salisbury', N'MD', N'21801');
INSERT INTO #Ids (Label, HandlerSourceId) VALUES (N'C1', CAST (SCOPE_IDENTITY () AS INT));
GO

-- Hoisted into a variable rather than written inline: PRINT takes a scalar EXPRESSION and rejects a subquery outright
-- (error 1046), and because that is a COMPILE error it kills the whole batch -- so writing it inline in the batch above
-- would have taken the CREATE TABLE down with it, and the failure would arrive as `Invalid object name '#Ids'` in
-- thirteen later assertions instead of here. Measured, not guessed at.
DECLARE @IdCount INT = (SELECT COUNT (*) FROM #Ids);
IF @IdCount = 4
    PRINT N'fixture: 4 row(s) inserted, 4 HandlerSourceId(s) captured into #Ids.';
ELSE
    PRINT CONCAT (N'FAIL fixture: expected 4 ids in #Ids, got ', @IdCount
                , N'. Every assertion below takes its argument from here, so nothing later is meaningful.');
GO

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
